 {"id":"14a1432f8d3a8b5cb828feec2bb84908dec33a1a30913ea960fc44a0ed955f63-0","type":"EXPEDITION_REFINED","timestamp":491,"transactionId":"14a1432f8d3a8b5cb828feec2bb84908dec33a1a30913ea960fc44a0ed955f63","capability":"RefineExpedition","actor":"synth-cli","payload":{"expeditionId":"f0912d1aeee16213","id":"f0912d1aeee16213","status":"draft","note":"\n============================================================\nFOLD 2026-10-14 (OPERATOR DECISION) — draft 31a26dadaf47a42d, \"Adapter configuration consolidated:\ndeclared vs detected\", is folded into this expedition and cancelled as a standalone draft.\n============================================================\nReason: it is the same defect class this expedition exists to end — names and configuration carried\nforward and acted on without asking the system what is factually true — and it had already diagnosed\ntoday's incident before it happened. Its record is retained (cancelled with a reason, not deleted);\nits 4579-char goal stays readable at .synth/data/expeditions/31a26dadaf47a42d.json.\n\nD7 (NEW DELIVERABLE). Adapter configuration has no home: five places, no authority order, no schema.\n  (a) .synth/config.yaml holds one `git:` block parsed AD HOC BY THREE INDEPENDENT MODULES\n      (ledger/branch.ts:74, adapter/git-snapshot.ts:338, repository/branch-policy.ts) with no type,\n      no loader module and no validation, so an unknown or misspelled key is silently ignored.\n  (b) `synth init` writes detection results into the generated manifest under\n      adapters: {selected, config, diagnostics} (cli/synth.ts:3070-3090) — a regenerable document,\n      and absent from this repository's manifest altogether.\n  (c) .synth/context.json persists a repository classification (repositoryType, confidence,\n      derivedFrom) but no adapter configuration.\n  (d) `synth adapter configure repository path=. remote=origin defaultBranch=main promotionMode=direct`\n      (cli/adapter.ts:11,74-86) is ACCEPTED AND THEN LOST: GitRepositoryAdapter.configure\n      (adapters/repository/git.ts:187-199) merges into in-memory state and never writes, so the verb\n      has no durable effect across a process restart.\n  (e) Forge credentials are split between env GITHUB_TOKEN and a config schema that declares `token`\n      required (adapters/github/adapter.ts:72), which invites a personal access token into tracked YAML.\n  Measured consequence, not theoretical: detectRecommendedAdapters already computes defaultBranch and\n  remote (cli/detect-adapters.ts:113-119) and BOTH ARE DROPPED, so the same fact is hardcoded three\n  times — ledger/delivery.ts:347 defaults baseBranches to [\"origin/main\",\"main\"] and no caller ever\n  passes the option; ledger/branch.ts:49 LEDGER_FALLBACK_BRANCH = \"main\"; cli/adapter.ts:81\n  defaultBranch || \"main\".\n\nD7 REQUIRED OUTCOMES (from the folded draft, unchanged).\n  1. A typed `adapters:` section in .synth/config.yaml — adapters.repository.{provider,remote,\n     baseBranch,snapshots,hooks} and adapters.forge.{provider,owner,repo,baseUrl} — read through ONE\n     new loader module with schema validation that warns on unknown keys.\n  2. Detected observations written to .synth/cache/adapters.json (gitignored, artifact class `cache`\n     per state/artifact-policy.ts:58, same precedent as dependency-records.json), each carrying value,\n     confidence and derivedFrom, and including a git BINARY probe so that \"git is not installed\" is\n     distinguishable from \"git is installed but this directory is not a repository\". Today\n     first-contact onboard:detect returns byte-identical output for both and advises `synth adapter\n     init` even when git is absent — advice the operator cannot follow.\n  3. A documented and tested authority order: CLI flag, then config.yaml, then cached detection, then\n     built-in default — where the built-in default is provider `none` declaring no capabilities,\n     NEVER origin/main.\n  4. The three hardcoded branch defaults deleted and fed from configuration through the baseBranches\n     option ledger/delivery.ts:347 already accepts.\n  5. `synth adapter configure --approve` promotes a detection proposal into config.yaml, plan-then-\n     approve in the same shape as first-contact onboard --approve; one mechanism makes the greenfield\n     offer and the brownfield offer the same code path.\n  6. Credentials resolved from env or a secret store only, with `token` removed from the GitHub\n     adapter's required config schema.\n  7. The existing `git:` block migrated to adapters.repository.* with a deprecation warning on read,\n     and `git.ledgerBranch` RETIRED: the ledger branch inferred from the checkout when it matches\n     branchPolicy.declaredPatterns, with trunk as the LAST resort rather than the first.\n  8. The ledger-branch fallback order corrected: a checkout matching a declared pattern must win over\n     trunk.\n\nOUTCOMES 7 AND 8 ARE NO LONGER HYPOTHETICAL — THEY HAPPENED TODAY, ON THIS BRANCH.\n  Observed 2026-10-14 while starting EXP-CLI-003: git.ledgerBranch in .synth/config.yaml named\n  arena/15e0fc51-synth (the PREVIOUS session's branch, absent from this checkout) and\n  .synth/data/ledger.json recorded an even older arena/c5db653e-synth. With both stale, resolution\n  fell back to local `main`, exactly as the config comment and the folded draft both predict, so the\n  ceremony commits for EXPEDITION_STARTED (23ad2d0c) and EXPEDITION_REFINED (cc669118) were written\n  onto the TRUNK and local main diverged from origin/main — by 2 commits then, by 6 earlier in the\n  same session (ad7849e, 73d532b, c35bc3e, 915eca0, 62422b9, 26a9997).\n  Recovery performed: the ledger content was consolidated onto the session branch (d5c639b), local\n  main was reset to origin/main (12884da), and a9cd6e3 set ledgerBranch to arena/d5e4625c-synth —\n  which already matches branchPolicy.declaredPatterns [\"arena/*\"]. That is the WORKAROUND the config\n  comment prescribes (\"Naming the checkout explicitly is the current workaround\"), not the fix: the\n  key is still session-scoped inside tracked config and is stale by construction the moment a session\n  ends. Outcomes 7 and 8 are the fix, and this expedition now owns them.\n\nORDERING INSIDE THIS EXPEDITION. D7 is a PREREQUISITE for D1, not a parallel item. D1 drives the\n  commit path through GitRepositoryAdapter, but outcome (d) above shows the adapter cannot durably\n  hold configuration today — configure() merges in memory and never writes. Consolidating\n  configuration and giving the adapter a durable, typed home must land before the commit path is\n  routed through it, or the new verb inherits the same silent-fallback behaviour.\n  Cross-link: EXP-CLEAN-001 D6(b) wants ONE authority for what a ceremony commit may CONTAIN; D7\n  wants ONE authority for WHICH BRANCH it lands on and where configuration comes from. Same\n  single-authority theme, different axis — coordinate so the two do not each grow a private resolver.\n\nSCOPE EXTENSION REQUIRED. `expedition refine` cannot change scope, so this must be applied when the\n  expedition is approved or committed. Add the folded draft's paths to this expedition's scope:\n  src/config/**, .synth/config.yaml, src/cli/detect-adapters.ts, src/cli/adapter.ts,\n  src/cli/first-contact.ts, src/adapters/repository/types.ts, src/adapters/github/adapter.ts,\n  src/adapters/adapter-catalog.ts, src/ledger/branch.ts, src/adapter/git-snapshot.ts,\n  src/repository/branch-policy.ts, src/state/artifact-policy.ts.\n  (Current scope already covers src/cli/**, src/ledger/**, src/adapters/repository/**, src/infra/**,\n  tests/**, data/tasks/**, docs/adr/**, AGENTS.md.)\n\nACCEPTANCE ADDED FROM THE FOLDED DRAFT.\n  * The three-system matrix — no git at all / git installed without a repository / git installed with\n    a repository — yields distinct, truthful recognition output and identical ledger behaviour in all\n    three.\n  * No repository or branch value is hardcoded outside the loader's documented defaults; the three\n    hardcoded branch defaults are gone.\n  * `synth adapter configure` survives a process restart.\n  * No secret can be written into .synth/config.yaml.\n  * The ledger records ceremonies with zero VCS present, reporting verification as UNAVAILABLE rather\n    than failing.\n  * A ceremony commit lands on the checkout that matches branchPolicy.declaredPatterns, never on\n    trunk, with no session-scoped branch name stored in tracked config.\n\n============================================================\nMEASURED EVIDENCE 2026-10-14 — a verb created and checked out a branch it was told not to create,\nand the ledger recorded a branch name that exists nowhere\n============================================================\nObserved while starting EXP-CLI-003 (e47a186cb472a20a) on this session branch. Reproduced, then\ndiagnosed from code and event offsets rather than inferred.\n\nWHAT RAN\n  synth expedition commit --proposal-id e47a186cb472a20a --branch arena/d5e4625c-synth --no-create\n  -> rc=0, and its own output reported executionBranch {\"branch\":\"arena/d5e4625c-synth\",\"created\":false}\n  -> the reflog simultaneously recorded: \"checkout: moving from arena/d5e4625c-synth to\n     expedition/framework-maturation-v2-4ab7e9d/exp-cli-003-make-the-light-path-light-and-enforce-the-load-budgets-e47a186\"\n  The output contradicted the reflog. Both were telling the truth about different code paths.\n\nTWO INDEPENDENT BRANCH PATHS INSIDE ONE VERB\n  Path A — ADR-056 declaration, src/repository/branch-declaration.ts:92-112. Reads --branch and\n    --no-create. Because currentBranch already equalled the declared branch it did nothing, and\n    emitted event 485 EXPEDITION_BRANCH_BOUND {branch: arena/d5e4625c-synth, created: false,\n    baseCommit: 189f7029ac21}. Correct.\n  Path B — the ECOSYSTEM-001 canonical-branch auto-create, src/cli/synth.ts ~6552-6599, INSIDE\n    cmdExpeditionCommit (which spans 6423..6923). Its own comment says \"Auto-create the canonical\n    expedition branch at start... When it runs, it switches to the canonical branch\". It computes\n    canonical = candidates[0] and calls adapter.branchExists / adapter.checkout(canonical) /\n    adapter.createBranch(canonical). IT NEVER READS noCreate OR declaredBranch; its only guards are\n    policy.mode === \"enforce\" && policy.strategy === \"featured\" and whether the current branch is\n    already a canonical candidate. It emitted event 486 EXPEDITION_BRANCH_CREATED for the canonical\n    name at the same baseCommit.\n  Events 485 and 486 are adjacent offsets, which is what proves both paths fired in one invocation\n  rather than the creation coming from a later command.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: A SELF-INFLICTED DEADLOCK\n  The ledger then bound execution to arena/d5e4625c-synth (Path A) while the checkout stood on the\n  canonical branch (Path B). The next `expedition start` failed BRANCH_POLICY_DENIED: \"expedition\n  execution is bound to arena/d5e4625c-synth (ADR-056); current branch is expedition/framework-...\n  Required branch: arena/d5e4625c-synth\" — denying the transition because the verb had itself moved\n  the checkout off the branch the ledger declared.\n  Operator note, recorded honestly: the SECOND creation (event 487) was my error, not the framework's\n  — I ran `expedition start --id e47a186c` bare, although ADR-056 §2.1 documents\n  `expedition start --id ... --branch <name>` and cmdExpeditionStart does read flags.branch and\n  flags[\"no-create\"]. Run correctly, `start --id e47a186cb472a20a --branch arena/d5e4625c-synth\n  --no-create` returned ok with executionBranch {created: false, declared: true}, did NOT switch\n  branches, did NOT create anything, emitted event 488 EXPEDITION_BRANCH_BOUND, and moved the\n  expedition to executing (autoCommit 23ad2d0c). So the deadlock is escapable — but only by an\n  operator who knows to repeat the declaration on a second verb, after the first verb already\n  ignored it once.\n\nA BRANCH NAME RECORDED THAT EXISTS NOWHERE\n  The canonical name is derived by slugifying mission + subject + id8\n  (canonicalBranchCandidates, src/repository/branch-taxonomy.ts:133) and then materialized to make\n  the derivation true. It was recorded twice (events 486 and 487, baseCommits 189f7029ac21 and\n  1423d53d6611). It was never pushed: `git ls-remote --heads origin | grep -c exp-cli-003` = 0. It no\n  longer exists locally (deleted twice during recovery). And the expedition record itself never\n  mentions it — 0 occurrences of the string in .synth/data/expeditions/e47a186cb472a20a.json — so the\n  contradiction lives only in the event stream, where nothing reconciles BOUND against CREATED.\n  This is the operator's diagnosis confirmed: the branch layer GUESSES names from strings and creates\n  reality to match, instead of asking git what exists. The hash layer is the only factual part, which\n  is exactly why containment-by-tipCommit worked for 4 of 4 sealed expeditions while every\n  name-based path in this session either failed (DELIVERY_PUSH_FAILED) or needed a human to go read\n  GitHub (--merge-commit).\n\nWHAT THIS EXPEDITION MUST THEREFORE DO, BEYOND ITS ORIGINAL DELIVERABLES\n  * One authority for branch decisions. A verb must not contain two paths that can disagree; if a\n    declared branch exists, the canonical auto-create path must not run, and `--no-create` must bind\n    every path in the verb, not just the one that implements it.\n  * A verb's output must describe what it DID, not what one of its paths intended. Returning\n    created:false while a checkout happened is worse than either behaviour alone, because it sends\n    the operator looking for the wrong cause.\n  * Never record a branch name that was not verified to exist, and never leave BOUND and CREATED\n    events unreconciled. If a name is derived, either verify it against git or do not write it.\n  * Declaration must not have to be repeated per verb. `--branch`/`--no-create` given at commit time\n    is already in the ledger (EXPEDITION_BRANCH_BOUND); `start` should read that binding instead of\n    requiring the operator to restate it, and should refuse to switch away from a bound branch.\nSequencing per operator instruction 2026-10-14: 'Draft the expedition with the premise that we will fix the CLI verbs before.' EXP-CLI-003 replaces the 9121-line switch with a verb -> loader registry, so 'consolidate' is registered there rather than added to the monolith and then moved. EXP-LIFE-001 (73c5c033360fecf3) is the source of findings A, B, D, G, H and J used in this goal but is deliberately NOT a dependency: it is an audit expedition that may never execute, and blocking on it would block this work indefinitely.","refinementId":"d0badd1308eedd76","refinedAt":491,"metadata":{"identity":{"agentId":"synth-cli-3422","sessionId":"e259cbfe-c053-458e-a378-dbaa19660501","approvalMode":"autonomous","issuedAt":491}}},"previousHash":"c4cee9e8be36aa2a8020f660c41894a5cc7810f7fa74026fec176f4b3de648b2","eventHash":"b41881bc5b449e8edaa6b6dada38a38214e07a92ebe9b4fde49810ea41d1672b","partitionKey":"EXPEDITION_REFINED","partition":3,"offset":492}
 {"id":"8807e8694a0680d2b05c798a4a2fb0b9f03bc0ae0bde70dbe5dbe8621a5a538e-0","type":"EXPEDITION_CANCELLED","timestamp":492,"transactionId":"8807e8694a0680d2b05c798a4a2fb0b9f03bc0ae0bde70dbe5dbe8621a5a538e","capability":"CancelExpedition","actor":"synth-cli","payload":{"expeditionId":"31a26dadaf47a42d","id":"31a26dadaf47a42d","status":"cancelled","reason":"Folded into EXP-LIFE-002 (f0912d1aeee16213) by operator decision 2026-10-14. Same defect class: names and configuration carried forward without factual verification. All five configuration sites, the measured consequence (detectRecommendedAdapters computes defaultBranch and remote at cli/detect-adapters.ts:113-119 and both are dropped, so the fact is hardcoded three times), the eight required outcomes and the acceptance matrix are reproduced in that expedition's refinement note as deliverable D7, where outcomes 7 and 8 are now backed by an observed incident rather than a prediction: git.ledgerBranch named the previous session's branch, resolution fell back to local main, and eight ceremony commits landed on the trunk during this session. This record is retained, not deleted; its 4579-char goal remains the detailed source.","cancelledAt":492,"metadata":{"identity":{"agentId":"synth-cli-3475","sessionId":"21e9a5f0-f233-4f74-a5fd-90d20eb584a7","approvalMode":"autonomous","issuedAt":492}}},"previousHash":"b41881bc5b449e8edaa6b6dada38a38214e07a92ebe9b4fde49810ea41d1672b","eventHash":"8cfb591e0a166b5fd5fe3bea54e9792eb4cd11446aa43bd5b974b1e44655519f","partitionKey":"EXPEDITION_CANCELLED","partition":3,"offset":493}
 {"id":"6d17dd172647401c3a0362311510a1e8b3137bfa68fbd20118392f793481741b-0","type":"EXPEDITION_REFINED","timestamp":500,"transactionId":"6d17dd172647401c3a0362311510a1e8b3137bfa68fbd20118392f793481741b","capability":"RefineExpedition","actor":"synth-cli","payload":{"expeditionId":"3ba2a3f886e87d29","id":"3ba2a3f886e87d29","status":"draft","note":"AMENDMENT to the EXP-CLEAN-001 inventory (operator instruction 2026-10-14: add the artifact-policy\nleak, commit the measurement tooling so the numbers are reproducible by anyone, and \"make it right\"\non the tsconfig flags inside this expedition). Four additions and two corrections. Nothing above is\nrewritten; the original measured goal stands as written.\n\nD6. ARTIFACT-POLICY LEAK — test byproducts are written into a tracked, committable directory, and a\n    ceremony commit then sweeps them into history. Three parts, all measured:\n      (a) src/cli/certification-runner.ts:423 writes `certification-report-${Date.now()}.json` into\n          options.outputDir, and tests/certification-framework.test.js:44 ASSERTS that a report file\n          was written (\"explain mode must write a report file\"). That test is chained, so every full\n          `npm run test:all` leaves three new untracked timestamped files under\n          proof/certifications/. Observed twice in two consecutive sessions (1791937544354/5/6 and\n          1791969422089/8/9), cleaned both times.\n      (b) Commit 5907889 (\"EXPEDITION_REFINED expedition 2c13ca22\") committed three of them —\n          526 lines each, 1578 lines of byproduct — alongside 3 ledger files. Mechanism:\n          src/ledger/ceremony.ts:339-366 builds `existing` from the pathspecs its CALLER passes and\n          commits with `git commit -m <msg> -- ...existing`. That caller's list included\n          proof/certifications/, which is NOT in AUTO_COMMIT_INCLUDE_ROOTS\n          (src/state/artifact-policy.ts:105 = [\".synth/data\", \"proof/expeditions\", \"AGENTS.md\"]).\n          So there are two competing definitions of ceremony-commit scope and the broader one won.\n      (c) scripts/check-artifact-policy.js PASSES over this (2195 tracked paths, \"rules durable:13\n          input:1 derived:1 cache:4 legacy:2\"), because the reports classify as committable. No\n          existing guard catches either half of this.\n    Deliverable: make certification reports write to a temp dir (or classify proof/certifications as\n    derived and ignore it), collapse ceremony-commit scope to ONE authority, and decide whether the\n    1578 lines already in history under proof/certifications/ come back out. This is the same\n    invariant class as \"reads never write\" and \"derived state never blocks work\" from the\n    artifact-policy expedition; it is a leak of exactly what those invariants forbid.\n\nD7. THE MEASUREMENT TOOLING IS NOW COMMITTED (774a322), so every number in this goal is\n    reproducible by anyone rather than only by the session that measured it. The four throwaway\n    scripts in /tmp did not survive a sandbox reset.\n      npm run audit:module-graph       eager graph per entry point + unreachable modules, reported\n                                       BOTH from the shipped roots (bin + main) and with\n                                       dist/cli/synth.js added as a harness root\n      npm run audit:unused-exports     exported names no other src file, test or script mentions,\n                                       each labelled barrel-exposed or not\n      npm run audit:dead-declarations  tsc --noEmit --noUnusedLocals --noUnusedParameters, reported\n                                       by diagnostic code and worst file; --max N turns it into a gate\n      npm run measure:cli-startup      median-of-N wall timings with a mandatory untouched control;\n                                       --compare A.json B.json prints control-adjusted deltas\n    All four are registered as tasks in data/tasks/ and routed through scripts/task-adapter-shim.js,\n    because tests/task-npm-adapter.test.js (chained, segment ~95) requires every npm script to\n    delegate to the task engine — adding them as direct node invocations turned the chain red.\n    They report rather than gate and are deliberately NOT chained: a gate that fails on the day it is\n    added teaches people to ignore gates. Known side effect: four unchained tasks take\n    `synth task doctor`'s orphaned-entry-point count from 151 to 155, the same warning class as the\n    115 unreachable orphan test files that EXP-TEST-005 owns.\n    Two self-checks are built in, because the original measurement was wrong twice before it was\n    right: audit-module-graph refuses to report when fewer than half of dist/ is reachable (the\n    signature of a resolver bug — one such bug reported 3 of 348 reachable and looked like a\n    catastrophic finding rather than an absolute-vs-relative path-key mismatch), and\n    audit-unused-exports fails if three known-used symbols appear as candidates (an earlier version\n    counted a docs/*.md mention as a reference and reported zero dead modules — wrong in the\n    comfortable direction, which would have hidden the finding entirely).\n\nD8. THE TSCONFIG FLAGS ARE A NAMED ACCEPTANCE GATE, NOT A SUB-ITEM (operator: \"let's change that,\n    make it right in the expedition we created for it\"). The finished state is all three of:\n      1. all 212 compiler-reported dead declarations removed (181 TS6133, 26 TS6196, 3 TS6138,\n         2 TS6192, across 100 files);\n      2. `noUnusedLocals: true` and `noUnusedParameters: true` present in tsconfig.json permanently,\n         so `npm run build` itself refuses to be green with dead code in it;\n      3. `audit:dead-declarations --max 0` chained into test:all as the guard against either the\n         count growing again or the flags being removed — at which point the flags make the script\n         redundant for src/ and it survives only as a guard on the flags.\n    A ratchet is available IMMEDIATELY and does not need this expedition: chaining\n    `audit:dead-declarations --max 212` today fails only if the count GROWS. Sequencing constraint\n    that must be respected: 17 of the 212 are in src/cli/synth.ts, which EXP-CLI-003 is decomposing,\n    so fixing them before that lands means fixing them twice. Order is CLI-003, then this.\n\nD9. TWO MEASURED CORRECTIONS to numbers in the goal above.\n      * The unreachable set is 44 modules / 2855 compiled lines under BOTH root sets — adding\n        dist/cli/synth.js as a fourth root reaches nothing the shipped entry points do not\n        (304 reachable either way). The goal assumed that adding it might shrink the candidate list;\n        measured with the committed resolver, it does not. The caveat still holds for computed\n        specifiers and direct test imports.\n      * Unused exports are 402, not 401: the committed resolver also parses `export { a, b as c }`\n        lists, which the throwaway version missed. 109 of the 402 are barrel-exposed, i.e. re-\n        exported wholesale by some index and therefore possibly public API of the published package.\n        Worst files unchanged: discovery/types.ts 21, ledger/delivery.ts 17,\n        cli/certification-runner.ts 11, mission-studio/types.ts 10, environment/rules.ts 9.\n\nCORRECTION OF MY OWN EARLIER CLAIM. On 2026-10-13 I reported that the ceremony auto-commit \"stages\nonly durable state paths\" and cited AUTO_COMMIT_INCLUDE_ROOTS as the authority, then relied on that\nto conclude `git add -N` was safe for untracked source files. The conclusion happened to be right\n(the source files were not swept in) but the premise was wrong: the ceremony writer commits the\npathspecs its caller passes, and 5907889 proves that list can include proof/certifications/. Anyone\nreasoning about what a ceremony commit will pick up must read the caller, not the constant.\n\n============================================================\nD10. SCOPE EXTENSION — FULL CLI VALUE AND COMPLEXITY AUDIT\nOperator instruction, 2026-10-14: \"extend scope on EXP-CLEAN-001 to do a full CLI audit and analize what is worth keeping, what can be done in a different way and what is totally overengineered.\"\n============================================================\nThis adds a product/workflow and architecture audit to the existing dead-code cleanup. It is not limited to unreachable modules, unused declarations, or the two zero-invocation verbs in D5. Code can be reachable and thoroughly tested yet still implement needless complexity. Conversely, absence from the test census is not proof that a command has no users or value. Preserve the original goal and prior refinements; this is an additive amendment, not an erasure of earlier decisions.\n\nAUDIT COVERAGE\n  1. Inventory the entire actual CLI at execution time: shipped entry point, compatibility entry point, every registered/public top-level verb, every implemented subcommand, aliases, flags and defaults, help/documentation exposure, hidden/legacy routes, and recursive CLI callers. Reconcile the registry, dispatch implementations and help rather than treating any one of them as a complete inventory. Recompute all historical counts after CLI-003 and the testing expeditions; do not treat the old 33-verb census or unused-code counts as current facts.\n  2. Follow real operator journeys, not just individual handlers: initialization/onboarding, planning and mission/expedition work, validation and governance, evidence and completion, delivery/seal, recovery, reporting, and adapter/task configuration. Record the necessary steps, required parameters, repeated information already held in state, failure/recovery paths and side effects. Look for unnecessary ceremonies and commands that force an operator to coordinate internal implementation details manually.\n  3. Trace implementation ownership for each surface: handler, shared helpers, authoritative state/configuration source, adapters, gates, persistence and Git/network effects. Identify duplicate authorities, redundant checks or abstractions, overlapping commands, excessive indirection, needless bootstrap/loading, inconsistent defaults and configuration that is accepted but not applied. Distinguish intentional safety invariants from accidental ceremony; simpler must not mean bypassing an actual integrity boundary.\n  4. Ground usage and compatibility claims in resolved imports/call sites, tasks/CI, tests, documentation, published API contracts and available operator evidence. Label unknown usage as unknown. A docs mention is not an executed call; a test-only caller is not proof of production use; no observed calls is not authorization to remove a public surface.\n\nREQUIRED DECISION REPORT\n  Produce a versioned, reproducible inventory and a decision matrix covering every inventoried command/subcommand. For each, record:\n    - user problem and value; implementation and consumer evidence;\n    - KEEP as-is, SIMPLIFY internally, CONSOLIDATE/REPLACE with an existing mechanism, REMOVE candidate, or INVESTIGATE with a named unresolved question;\n    - whether the design is proportionate to its job or overengineered, with specific evidence (not a label based only on file size, line count or personal preference);\n    - a simpler alternative where proposed: fewer steps/parameters, an existing verb, a shared internal service, or the proper adapter rather than another public command;\n    - tradeoffs, invariants preserved, compatibility and migration implications, tests affected, measurable expected benefit, decision owner and implementation owner.\n  Compare the current and proposed end-to-end workflows. Include before/after examples and a prioritized simplification plan. Explicitly identify redundant verbs and public concepts, and explain what remains necessary even when it is complex. Do not preselect a command-count reduction target or invent new verbs to make the audit appear productive.\n\nACCEPTANCE AND IMPLEMENTATION BOUNDARY\n  - No public or implemented route silently omitted from the inventory: differences among dispatch, help and docs are recorded and resolved or explicitly assigned.\n  - Every keep/remove/simplify judgment is traceable to evidence; heuristic candidates are confirmed before deletion. Unknowns and deferred findings have a reason and an owner rather than disappearing into a summary.\n  - Review the proposed target CLI and public keep/remove/rename/merge decisions with the operator BEFORE implementing those interface changes. The existing explicit decision to remove `migrate` stands; this amendment is not blanket approval to remove other commands.\n  - Execute confirmed dead-code removal and approved simplifications within CLEAN-001's scope; substantial new behavior or changes already owned elsewhere remain with that owner, rather than expanding cleanup into an unbounded rewrite. Preserve working contracts and safety invariants unless a change is explicitly approved and documented.\n  - Carry forward CLI-003's load-budget, entry-point parity, real-count and no-new-test-regression guards. Retain the permanent compiler/removal guards required by D8 and the original goal. Record before/after measurements and evidence; do not claim a speedup from incomparable runs.\n  - Produce the full audit and decision report even where the recommendation is to KEEP a component. A list of deleted files alone does not satisfy this scope extension.\n\nSEQUENCING AND EXISTING OWNERSHIP\n  Existing hard dependencies and draft status remain unchanged: CLI-003 first, testing work before code cleanup. This amendment does not start or approve CLEAN-001 and does not widen CLI-003 into a general CLI redesign.\n  Reconcile overlapping work explicitly: the governance-cluster audit (761c9be33f165253), legacy-verb folding (29b9a3704eb1287c), the src/scripts DRY drafts (e38aa99f7f2593af / 9ab1218c6f872c60), and the older legacy-removal expedition (912a468ae9bf5bd6). Reuse their findings and record ownership boundaries; do not silently cancel them, duplicate their implementation, or assume a historical inventory is current.\n  LIFE-002 (f0912d1aeee16213) retains ownership of expedition lifecycle/commit consolidation and adapter-configuration consolidation. This audit may assess the resulting experience and report gaps, but must not build a competing commit authority or another configuration loader.\n\nD6 OWNERSHIP CLARIFICATION — preserve the earlier finding, correct the delivery assignment\n  D6(b)'s single ceremony-commit scope authority is implemented by LIFE-002, as already specified in its charter; CLEAN-001 verifies and consumes that result rather than implementing it a second time. CLEAN-001 retains the certification-test byproduct/output-location and artifact-policy guard work in D6(a)/(c).\n  The operator's existing decision is to KEEP the 1578 lines of already-committed certification byproducts. The earlier text asking whether to remove those historical reports is therefore resolved: do not delete them as cleanup. Prevent future accidental accumulation instead. No additional dependency is silently added by this clarification; if unfinished LIFE-002 work blocks the relevant acceptance check, record that dependency explicitly before implementation.\n","refinementId":"9a12df54fd58a57c","refinedAt":500,"metadata":{"identity":{"agentId":"synth-cli-3819","sessionId":"179612e5-008d-4d88-a1a6-a037c8d71cf3","approvalMode":"autonomous","issuedAt":500}}},"previousHash":"6567acba5164f1de83aae5cd50d7dbd18cc3012e6043565fc12762ef75233c6a","eventHash":"ad8750205022104c22cbf83e7d17401e32ddfaa5ea13b2ad59ebbc00365ff449","partitionKey":"EXPEDITION_REFINED","partition":3,"offset":501}
